# flag (the 9.9 gap): Go's package; NewFlagSet's default Usage holds the FlagSet through a weak pointer, so that a FlagSet is not a reference cycle.

@note NewFlagSet sets `f.Usage = f.defaultUsage`, a method value that holds the FlagSet, while the FlagSet holds the method value: a reference cycle (D1, ADR-004) that no FlagSet made by NewFlagSet would ever be freed from (every program with its own flag set leaked a few hundred bytes, and LeakSanitizer reported it). The patch makes the default `Usage` a function that holds a weak pointer to the FlagSet and calls `defaultUsage` while the FlagSet is alive. `FlagSet.Usage` is still a non-nil func, as in Go, so the programs that read and wrap it (`old := fs.Usage; fs.Usage = func() { ...; old() }`) work. The one difference: a copy of the default `fs.Usage` called after the last reference to `fs` is gone does nothing, where Go prints the usage of a flag set that nothing else can reach. The command line's flag set (`CommandLine`) is not affected: its Usage is the plain function `commandLineUsage`.

@file flag.go
@why The method value `f.defaultUsage` holds f strongly and f holds it through f.Usage: a cycle. A weak pointer breaks it.
@replace
	f.Usage = f.defaultUsage
	return f
@with
	wp := weak.Make(f) // not f.defaultUsage: that method value would hold f, and f holds it: a reference cycle in gocpp
	f.Usage = func() {
		if p := wp.Value(); p != nil {
			p.defaultUsage()
		}
	}
	return f
@end

@why The weak pointer of NewFlagSet.
@replace
	"time"
)
@with
	"time"
	"weak"
)
@end
